<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Stool</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Stool is the sacred royal throne and symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, believed to contain the soul of the Asante nation. Tradition holds that it was brought down from the sky by the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-hodgson">Governor Hodgson</a>'s demand in 1900 that it be surrendered provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-stool.jpg" alt="Golden Stool" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UK gov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_stool_31_January_1935.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its full name is Sika Dwa Kofi, the Golden Stool born on a Friday. In tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a>, high priest and one of the two chief founders of the Asante Confederacy, made it descend from the sky onto the lap of the first king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>. Stools were customary signs of a chief's leadership, and the Golden Stool is held to house the spirit of the Asante nation, living, dead and yet to be born.</p><p>It is a curved seat 46 cm high, with a platform 61 cm wide and 30 cm deep. Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> stools it is carved from a single block of Alstonia boonei, a tall forest tree with numinous associations, and its whole surface is inlaid with gold and hung with bells to warn the king of danger. Few have seen it, and only the king, the queen and trusted advisers know where it is kept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A stool is understood as the seat of its owner's soul. The Golden Stool must never touch the ground and rests on a blanket. It is carried to the king on a pillow, since only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> may handle it, and at his inauguration a new king is raised and lowered over it without touching it. On solemn occasions it stands at the king's left on a throne of its own, the hwedom dwa. Replicas made for chiefs are blackened with animal blood at their funerals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many wars have been fought over the throne. In 1896 Asantehene Prempeh I accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> rather than risk losing both a war and the stool. Sir Frederick Hodgson, Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, demanded the right to sit on it and ordered a search, and the war that followed ended with the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> but left the stool untouched.</p><p>In 1921 African road workers found it and stripped some of its gold ornaments; tried by local custom, they were sentenced to death, but the British intervened and they were banished instead. After a British assurance of non-interference the stool came out of hiding, and in 1935 it was used to crown Osei Tutu Agyeman Prempeh II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golden Stool” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>No one sits on the <b>Golden Stool</b>: the Asantehene is enstooled beneath it, as it is the soul of the whole Asante nation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top the Asantehene sat on the <b>Golden Stool</b>, believed to hold the spirit of the nation, and governed with councils of the chiefs of the member states.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1900 the governor's demand for the <b>Golden Stool</b>, the sacred symbol of the Asante nation, provoked a rising led by Yaa Asantewaa, queen mother of Ejisu.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a></li></ul></section>
